{
  "title": "Gates on GitHub Actions 2: opt-in per repository, refusals, local fallback and baselines",
  "body": "## Problem\n\nPart of #310 (slice b). #861’s executor already exists in `src/gates/github-actions.ts`, but production does not select it.\n\nUploading candidates to Actions creates a publication boundary before the pipeline’s audit and delivery checks. Safe activation, mixed routing, fallback and reporting exceed one 250-line slice. This first slice establishes executor admission and fail-closed handling; production activation belongs to the follow-up below.\n\n## Requirements\n\n1. **Operator authorization.** Add optional boolean repository settings in operator `config.toml`, both defaulting to `false`:\n   ```toml\n   [repos.\"\u003cowner\u003e/\u003crepo\u003e\"]\n   github_actions = true\n   github_actions_paid_minutes = false\n   ```\n   Validate through `src/config.ts`; use its existing repository-name normalization and duplicate rejection. These settings authorize invocation but do not activate production selection in this slice. Keep `[gates].executor` restricted to its existing values. Older releases ignore these new repository keys with warnings and continue their existing routing; document this rollback behavior.\n\n2. **Authoritative destination.** Every Actions invocation requires a daemon-owned run/repository record, resolved independently of the request and candidate Git configuration. Bind authorization, API calls and explicit Git transport destinations to that identity; reject mismatched requests or handles. Validate GitHub repository identity and boolean visibility metadata before any push or dispatch. Unknown, malformed or unavailable visibility refuses remote execution. Direct invocation of a private repository requires both operator settings. Repository files, issue/comment text and model output cannot grant permission. Recheck authorization and cancellation before each remote side effect after intervening awaits.\n\n3. **Publication before upload.** Before either temporary-ref push, use the existing safeguards in `src/git/repos.ts` and `src/gates/publication.ts` to inspect the exact outgoing refs, commit metadata, paths and content, including intermediate commits and history newly exposed at the destination. Checking only `baseSha..headSha`’s net diff is insufficient. Include configured private strings and registered credentials; retain applicable discreet-mode safeguards and the executor’s existing CI-configuration refusal. Before upload, also validate all eventual dispatch inputs, including setup/check commands. Push and dispatch only the immutable SHAs and inputs inspected. Missing inspection or unsafe content refuses with **zero pushes and zero dispatches**. Never forward the daemon environment. Redact credentials and private text before diagnostic storage or truncation.\n\n4. **Fail closed; no infrastructure retry or local fallback.** Remove that feature from this issue’s contract, including the follow-up. Supervisor-recorded setup/check failures remain gate failures with normal feedback. Missing dispatches, incomplete/malformed/contradictory evidence, security refusals, cancellation and unconfirmed cleanup block verification; none permits local substitution or a clean pass. Never infer infrastructure status from candidate output text. Operational failures alone do not start an implementation round. Preserve the observed failure and classification.\n\n5. **Conservative baseline evidence.** Add optional, factory-authored per-result executor provenance in `src/gates/run.ts`, including retained attempts. An Actions-involving baseline must run fresh: bypass **both reads and writes** of the baseline cache in `src/pipeline/engine.ts`. An Actions baseline failure, or a baseline with missing/different execution identity, cannot make a candidate failure nonblocking `still_failing`. Preserve existing comparison behavior for legacy local-only results. Record the trusted workflow revision and `runs-on` label (`ubuntu-24.04` in the current workflow); the label is not an installed image version and does not establish cache equivalence. Actual-image discovery and Actions cache reuse are outside this slice.\n\n6. **Durable interruption handling.** Extend the existing handle/checkpoint path in `src/gates/executor.ts` and `src/pipeline/gate-execution.ts`, bound to repository, checked SHA and gate-configuration digest. Reuse completed matching checkpointed results. An interrupted Actions invocation must complete required cleanup and then remain durably blocked; restart must not automatically replace it or redispatch. Persist cleanup failures and retain the original executor identity until cleanup succeeds. Disabling authorization prevents new publication but preserves cleanup capability. Legacy Actions handles lacking the new binding are cleanup-only. Use optional serialized fields; no destructive migration.\n\n## Tests\n\nUse injected GitHub, push/delete dependencies and clock; no network, real `gh` or paid models. Extend focused existing tests. Demonstrate each new regression test fails when its corresponding implementation change is removed.\n\n1. **Authorization configuration:** parse defaults, explicit enablement, paid permission, invalid types and normalized duplicate repository names. A configured repository remains on existing production routing in this slice.\n2. **Destination and visibility:** authorized public and paid-private direct invocations push and dispatch successfully. Missing authorization, unpaid-private, unknown/malformed visibility, request/handle mismatch and hostile candidate remotes cause zero pushes and dispatches. Assert every successful API/transport destination matches the independently recorded repository. Revoke permission during a deferred lookup and assert no subsequent publication.\n3. **Publication boundary:** independently place protected text or credentials in a filename, blob, commit metadata, intermediate commit and dispatch command; include supported encoded forms and an inspection failure. Each case causes zero pushes and dispatches and sanitized persisted diagnostics. A clean control dispatches exactly once, using the inspected SHAs/inputs without daemon environment values.\n4. **Failure classification:** deliberately failing candidate setup remains blocking and reaches normal feedback. Missing dispatch, malformed/contradictory records, incomplete commands and cleanup failure remain operationally blocked. Assert no infrastructure redispatch, local execution or implementation feedback caused solely by the operational failure.\n5. **Baseline safety:** through preparation/cache wiring, seed a passing local cache entry and assert an Actions baseline executes without reading or writing it. Compare an Actions baseline failure with a genuine local candidate regression, and the reverse; neither becomes nonblocking. Also cover missing/different identity and retained-attempt provenance.\n6. **Restart boundaries:** reopen persisted state before dispatch, after dispatch intent, during cleanup and after completed-result checkpointing. Assert no replacement dispatch, durable cleanup failure, blocked interrupted work and reuse of matching completed results. Changed SHA/configuration and legacy unbound handles cannot replay results; authorization removal still allows cleanup.\n\n## Out of scope\n\n- Production Actions selection, mixed routing and report/feed rendering: follow-up slice only.\n- Automatic infrastructure retry/local fallback, Actions baseline caching and actual runner-image discovery.\n- Workflow/executor redesign, LAN workers (#165), local-executor redesign (#784), cloud workers, sharding and agents’ own tests (#150).\n- No workflow edits or new publication allowances.\n\n## Budget\n\nTarget **220–250 changed source lines**, counting additions and deletions across runtime code and SQL; **250 is a hard stop**. Tests and concise documentation are additional. Reuse existing publication, checkpoint and serialization helpers. If the safety contract cannot fit, stop and rescope before production activation; do not omit a guard to meet the cap.\n\n## Follow-up slice\n\n- **Activation and mixed routing:** target 220–250 changed source lines, hard stop 250. Operator authorization selects Actions for base-commit `[gates].remote_checks`: absent means `[\"test\"]`, `[]` means none, invalid types/unknown names reject configuration. Other checks retain local/LAN/in-place routing; run prerequisites in every used environment. Apply consistently to baselines, existing check retries, post-merge verification and land checks, including land entries without a run. Restore missing legacy settings from their recorded trusted base revision.\n- **Durable presentation:** retain optional actual executor and confirmed CI run ID/link per result and attempt; derive links from trusted identities. Render report and actual feed-producer data, deduplicated across restart. Legacy missing provenance remains readable; any required migration must be additive. Keep Actions cache bypass and the prohibition on infrastructure retry/fallback.\n- **Numbered acceptance tests:** **7.** Enabled public and paid-private production runs dispatch exactly the selected checks; default-off, unpaid-private and `[]` produce zero remote publication. Cover mixed checks/setup, base-versus-candidate configuration and every entry point above. **8.** Reopen persisted mixed results and verify report/feed provenance, trusted links and no duplicate feed items. Mutation-check both tests. Ship only when the complete activation contract fits its cap.\n- **Routing acceptance (follow-up test 7 must also cover):** with Actions authorized and `remote_checks` absent, exactly `test` dispatches and other checks keep their existing routing. Table-test invalid types and unknown check names: configuration is rejected before any gate execution, push or dispatch. Reopen a legacy run that lacks the setting, give its recorded trusted base and its candidate conflicting settings, and assert that routing follows the recorded base; if that trusted revision can't be read, verification stays blocked with zero remote publication. Removing each default, validation or restoration behavior must fail its test.\n\n_Spec-reviewed twice on 2026-10-29: four SPEC-P1s resolved in the first revision; the second review's remaining SPEC-P2 (routing acceptance) is folded in above._\n"
}
